The Pythagoreans: number, proportion, and explanation (@greek-pythagoreans: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @greek-nature-being, #source @greek-source-fairbanks, #philosophy @greek-eleatic-arguments, #ancient-greek-philosophy @greek-eleatic-arguments, #philosophy @greek-heraclitus, #ancient-greek-philosophy @greek-heraclitus, #philosophy @greek-meno, #ancient-greek-philosophy @greek-meno) { r_2J6KPA7CCD3NY1G0VCAT35Q2NK
**Read:** [The First Philosophers of Greece](../sources/Presocratics-Fairbanks.lingua#r_0CETPRSWH0WZT8335D8692RNJY), Chapter IX, “Pythagoras and the Pythagoreans”. Compare the reports from Aristotle with the passages on number, limits, opposites, and harmony. Identify the ancient reporting author before attributing a claim to Pythagoras or to every member of the tradition.

**Portuguese alternative:** [Os pré-socráticos: fragmentos, doxografia e comentários, Nova Cultural](../sources/Presocratics-Nova-Cultural.lingua#r_6ZA1ENV6PB59E6EGC7MCBS1782). Use the corresponding thinker's section, keeping fragments, ancient reports, and commentary distinct. The guide's question can be investigated in either collection; identify the edition and passage when comparing them.

**Preparation:** ordinary counting and simple ratios. A ratio compares two quantities: doubling a length gives a ratio of two to one. Introduce any unfamiliar mathematical term with an example as it appears; a geometry course is not an entry requirement. [The reading method](../foundations/Read-an-argument.lingua#r_4GMQCKPBF9BCSKDK0KPN013EBN) supplies the distinction between a claim and its support.

Ask what an appeal to number explains: a measurable relation, an arrangement, the nature of a thing, or the order of the whole world. These are different claims. Distinguish a successful mathematical description from a further assertion that reality itself consists of numbers or their principles.

**Practice:** describe a repeated pattern with a count or ratio. State what the description lets you calculate, then identify an explanatory claim it does not yet justify. Compare this difference with a report in the chapter.

**Check:** reconstruct one ancient report, explain its use of number, and separate the reporting author's interpretation from evidence for the earlier position. Identify what would make your own comparison too strong.

**Crossings:** [Meno's geometry and inquiry](../socratic/Meno.lingua#r_6NC59NWAQ6A7YZYBJ3CG12SCTN) and [Zeno on division and plurality](Zeno-and-Melissus.lingua#r_5WR6CJM0JV5QPDX2P5177J0YT4). **Return:** [Pre-Socratic readings](Nature-and-being.lingua#r_61M87EN28QQ9AHXT80SPCB0CVH) · [Ancient Greek philosophy](../Ancient-Greek-Philosophy.lingua#r_464ZHR7ZYC234A0XX2QEW47VB3).
